## The Proxy Pattern in Java  
The **Proxy** pattern gives you an object that “stands in” for another object.  
Typical reasons to use a proxy:

| Reason | Example | Proxy type |
|--------|---------|------------|
| **Lazy‑loading** (create the real object only when it is really needed) | An image that is read from disk only when it is displayed | *Virtual Proxy* |
| **Access control** (restrict or guard calls) | Only authorized users can call certain methods | *Protection Proxy* |
| **Logging / auditing** | Log every method call to a remote service | *Logging Proxy* |
| **Remote access** (client talks to a server) | Client calls a remote object as if it were local | *Remote Proxy* |
| **Caching / memoization** | Cache results of expensive calculations | *Caching Proxy* |

---

## 1. The Classic (Static) Proxy

A **static proxy** is just a normal Java class that implements the same interface as the real subject and forwards calls to it.  
Because the proxy is written by hand you can add any extra logic you want.

```java
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1. Subject interface
// ---------------------------------------------------
public interface Image {
    void display();
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2. Real subject – the heavy thing we want to hide
// ---------------------------------------------------
public class RealImage implements Image {
    private final String fileName;

    public RealImage(String fileName) {
        this.fileName = fileName;
        loadFromDisk(fileName);
    }

    private void loadFromDisk(String fileName) {
        System.out.println("Loading image from disk: " + fileName);
    }

    @Override
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Displaying image: " + fileName);
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 3. Proxy – lazy loads the real subject
// ---------------------------------------------------
public class ProxyImage implements Image {
    private final String fileName;
    private RealImage realImage;          // initially null

    public ProxyImage(String fileName) {
        this.fileName = fileName;
    }

    @Override
    public void display() {
        if (realImage == null) {          // create on first use
            realImage = new RealImage(fileName);
        }
        realImage.display();              // delegate
    }
}
```

### Usage

```java
public class ProxyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Image img = new ProxyImage("largePicture.jpg");

        // Nothing loaded yet
        System.out.println("Image created – nothing loaded yet.");

        // Now we display it – the real image is loaded lazily
        img.display();   // prints loading + displaying
    }
}
```

**What you learn**  
* The proxy has the same public contract (`Image`) as the real object.  
* It keeps a reference to the real object and creates it only when needed.  
* You can add any side‑effects you want (caching, security checks, logging, etc.) before/after delegating to the real object.

---

## 2. Dynamic Proxy – `java.lang.reflect.Proxy`

Sometimes you don’t want to write a separate proxy class for each interface.  
JDK dynamic proxies let you create a proxy at runtime that implements any interface you choose and delegates to a handler that implements `InvocationHandler`.

```java
import java.lang.reflect.*;

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Subject interface
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
public interface Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b);
    int subtract(int a, int b);
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Real subject
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
public class RealCalculator implements Calculator {
    @Override public int add(int a, int b)      { return a + b; }
    @Override public int subtract(int a, int b) { return a - b; }
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. InvocationHandler that adds logging
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
public class LoggingHandler implements InvocationHandler {
    private final Object realObj;    // the actual object to delegate to

    public LoggingHandler(Object realObj) {
        this.realObj = realObj;
    }

    @Override
    public Object invoke(Object proxy, Method method, Object[] args)
            throws Throwable {

        System.out.println("[LOG] About to invoke: " + method.getName());
        Object result = method.invoke(realObj, args);   // delegate
        System.out.println("[LOG] Finished: " + method.getName() +
                           " -> result: " + result);
        return result;
    }
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Create the proxy
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
public class DynamicProxyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator realCalc = new RealCalculator();

        Calculator proxyCalc = (Calculator) Proxy.newProxyInstance(
            Calculator.class.getClassLoader(),
            new Class<?>[]{Calculator.class},
            new LoggingHandler(realCalc));

        // Use the proxy just like the real object
        int sum = proxyCalc.add(5, 7);          // logs around the call
        int diff = proxyCalc.subtract(10, 3);   // logs around the call
    }
}
```

**Why dynamic proxies are handy**

| Benefit | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **No boilerplate** – you don't write a new proxy class for each interface. |
| **Centralised behaviour** – all proxies can share a single `InvocationHandler`. |
| **Runtime flexibility** – you can change the behaviour by supplying a different handler. |

---

## 3. Class‑based proxies (CGLIB / ByteBuddy)

JDK dynamic proxies only work for interfaces.  
If you need to proxy a concrete class (e.g. an abstract class or a final class that cannot be proxied by interface), you can use byte‑code generation libraries such as **CGLIB** or **ByteBuddy**.

Example with CGLIB (illustrative; actual usage would need the library on the classpath):

```java
import net.sf.cglib.proxy.*;

public class CglibProxyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Enhancer enhancer = new Enhancer();
        enhancer.setSuperclass(RealImage.class);          // target class
        enhancer.setCallback((MethodInterceptor) (obj, method, args, proxy) -> {
            System.out.println("[CGLIB] Before " + method.getName());
            Object result = proxy.invokeSuper(obj, args);
            System.out.println("[CGLIB] After "  + method.getName());
            return result;
        });

        RealImage proxyImage = (RealImage) enhancer.create();
        proxyImage.display();
    }
}
```

**When to use class‑based proxies**

* The subject does **not** expose an interface.  
* You need to extend or replace a concrete class at runtime.  
* You’re already using a framework that offers such proxies (Spring AOP, Guice, etc.).

---

## 4. Real‑World Example: Remote Proxy

A classic use case is a **remote proxy** that represents an object that lives on a different machine.

```java
public interface RemoteFile {
    byte[] read(String path) throws IOException;
}

public class RemoteFileImpl implements RemoteFile {
    // The real implementation would talk to a server over sockets, RMI, gRPC, etc.
    @Override public byte[] read(String path) throws IOException {
        // Imagine network call here …
        System.out.println("Reading from remote server: " + path);
        return new byte[0]; // dummy
    }
}

// The proxy might add connection pooling, retries, or lazy connect
public class RemoteFileProxy implements RemoteFile {
    private RemoteFileImpl real;
    private final String serverAddress;

    public RemoteFileProxy(String serverAddress) {
        this.serverAddress = serverAddress;
    }

    private void connect() {
        if (real == null) {
            // Lazily create the real connection
            real = new RemoteFileImpl(); // in reality, would use serverAddress
            System.out.println("Connected to remote server at " + serverAddress);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public byte[] read(String path) throws IOException {
        connect();
        return real.read(path);
    }
}
```

This pattern lets the client code stay the same no matter whether the file lives locally or on a remote server.

---

## 5. Summary of the Proxy Pattern

| Pattern Element | What it is | Where it lives | Typical use |
|-----------------|------------|----------------|-------------|
| **Subject** | Interface (or abstract class) that declares the common operations. | Defined once. | The contract between client and proxy/real. |
| **Real Subject** | The actual implementation of `Subject`. | Implements the interface. | The heavy or remote object. |
| **Proxy** | Another implementation of `Subject`. | Holds a reference to the real subject (or creates it). | Adds extra behavior before/after delegating. |

**Common proxy tricks**

* **Lazy initialization** – create the real object only on first call.  
* **Access control** – check permissions before delegating.  
* **Logging / profiling** – log method calls or measure execution time.  
* **Caching** – remember results of expensive operations.  
* **Security** – encrypt/decrypt data passing to/from the real object.  

**When not to use a proxy**

* If the real object is cheap to create and does not need any special handling.  
* When you want to avoid the extra indirection overhead.  

---

## 6. Quick Code Snippet Cheat Sheet

```java
// --------------------- 1. Static Proxy ---------------------
interface Service { void execute(); }
class RealService implements Service { ... }
class ServiceProxy implements Service {
    private RealService real;
    public void execute() { if (real==null) real=new RealService(); real.execute(); }
}

// --------------------- 2. Dynamic Proxy ---------------------
class LoggingHandler implements InvocationHandler { ... }
Service proxy = (Service) Proxy.newProxyInstance(
    Service.class.getClassLoader(),
    new Class<?>[]{Service.class},
    new LoggingHandler(new RealService()));

// --------------------- 3. CGLIB Proxy (example) ---------------------
Enhancer enhancer = new Enhancer();
enhancer.setSuperclass(RealService.class);
enhancer.setCallback((MethodInterceptor) (obj, method, args, proxy) -> {
    System.out.println("before");
    Object res = proxy.invokeSuper(obj, args);
    System.out.println("after");
    return res;
});
Service cglibProxy = (Service) enhancer.create();
```

With this foundation you can create any kind of proxy that fits your use case. Happy coding!
